ready: 1
kind: rule
id: agents.rule.write-guard
display_id: RULE-AGENTS-WRITE-GUARD
module: agents
title: Agents do not write Kernle's files directly
summary: A tool call that would write, move, or delete anything inside the Kernle home is denied with a pointer to the memory tools.
inputs:
  - { name: tool name, source: The pre-tool hook payload, source_kind: payload }
  - { name: target paths, source: "File path fields, and path arguments of shell commands, resolved against the working directory and symlinks", source_kind: derived }
when: Any target path of a write, edit, move, or delete resolves inside the Kernle home
then: The call is denied with the reason "Kernle memory is changed through its tools. Use remember, forget, or update instead." and the file is not touched
otherwise: The guard passes the call on
constraints:
  - text: The guard is a convenience against accidents and instructions injected into an agent, not a security boundary; the owner's account can always reach the files.
  - text: Memory written by any process other than the daemon is never treated as owner-authored.
    hard: true
rationale: >-
  An agent, or text injected into it, could otherwise rewrite memory directly
  and bypass provenance, the trust gate, and review. Because shell commands can
  reach the home in ways no parser sees, the real protection is that the
  daemon owns the database and never grants owner trust to content it did not
  receive from the owner's own commands.
